<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ash Shihr</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Al-Shihr</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ash Shihr is a port town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> coast of Hadramawt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mukalla">Mukalla</a>. For centuries a chief port of Hadramawt for frankincense, horses and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean-trade">Indian Ocean trade</a>, it gave its name to the whole coast in medieval Arabic geography. Long held by the Kathiri sultans, it passed to the Qu'aiti dynasty in the 19th century and was their seat before Mukalla.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ash-shihr.jpg" alt="Ash Shihr" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: renato agostini · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Passeggiata_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ash Shihr, also written al-Shihr, is a walled town on a sandy beach in Hadhramaut. It has an anchorage but no docks, so boats are used. A dry riverbed, the wadi al-Misyal, divides the town in two: the western quarter is called Majraf and the eastern al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramla">Ramla</a>. It has several souqs, among them Suq al-Lakham, Suq al-Hunud and Suq Shibam. Fish oil was its main export.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Its history can be traced back to about 780 CE, and it was a major port on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> trade route, exporting frankincense as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and Ibn Khurradadhbih called the area around it the bilad al-kundur, the Land of Incense. It was also known for its ambergris. Pottery made at Yadhghat was exported through it, possibly as early as the 10th century. In 980 Persian exiles founded the rival port of Sharma up the coast, and medieval geographers from al-Muqaddasi in 985 to al-Dimashqi around 1300 mention the two together. It remained the main port of Hadhramaut until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mukalla">Mukalla</a> replaced it in the 19th century.</p><p>Politically the town was held by the Ziyadids (818 to 981), the Banu Man in the 11th century, the Rasulids (1228 to 1454) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahirid-dynasty">Tahirids</a>, and was taken in 1462 by the Kathiri sultanate under Badr ibn Tuwayriq. The Portuguese, who called it Xaer or Xael, attacked it several times in the 16th century, with battles in 1523, 1532, 1533 and 1548. In 1530 the Ottomans Mustafa Bey and Khoja Zufar advised Badr not to submit to the Portuguese and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> and 100 levends with him. In 1548 the Portuguese battered the fort with siege guns and stormed it on 7 April, slaughtering the Kathiri garrison. Later it became one of the three major cities of the Qu'aiti Sultanate, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mukalla">Mukalla</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shibam">Shibam</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Graves along the shore are said to be those of victims of the Portuguese. Carsten Niebuhr visited the town in the 18th century. Yemenis who emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, and their descendants, are known as Shihiris, because most of them left through the port of al-Shihr.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Shihr” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>With Indian wealth they bought land and influence in Hadramawt, taking Shibam and the port of <b>Shihr</b> from around 1858, then Mukalla from the Kasadi Sultanate in 1881.</blockquote><a href="/state/quaiti-sultanate">Qu'aiti Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
